'''Nagato Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|長門県}}, ''Nagato-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nankai]] Region on the southern coast of the island of [[Yagikoshu]], facing the [[Shendan Ocean]]. With a population of 892,079 as of 2026, it is a prefecture of dramatic coastal scenery and deep historical resonance. The prefectural capital is the city of [[Nagato]], a port and former shogunal stronghold that controls the southern approaches to the Yagikoshu interior.

Nagato Prefecture is defined by two things: its {{nihongo|paper-making tradition|和紙|washi}}, which produces some of the finest hand-made paper in Adonia, and its {{nihongo|copper mining|銅山|dōzan}} heritage, which shaped the prefecture's economy and landscape for over 400 years. The prefecture is also known for its {{nihongo|fugu|河豚}}—pufferfish—which is caught in the rich waters off the southern coast and prepared by licensed chefs in the Nagato tradition.

== History ==

=== The copper kingdom ===
[[File:Kumagawa Copper Mine.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Kumagawa Copper Mine, one of the richest deposits in Yagikoshu (1572-1973)]]
The {{nihongo|Kumagawa Copper Mine|熊川銅山|Kumagawa Dōzan}}, discovered in 1572, was one of the richest copper deposits in Yagikoshu and the economic foundation of the Nagato lords for over three centuries. At its peak in the 1780s, the mine produced approximately 4,500 tonnes of copper annually—roughly 15% of Okaiken's total output—and the {{nihongo|Kumagawa Copper|熊川銅|Kumagawa-dō}} was used for coinage, temple bells, and the distinctive copper roofing of shogunal architecture.

The mine's wealth funded the construction of the {{nihongo|Nagato Castle|長門城|Nagato-jō}}, one of the largest fortresses in southern Yagikoshu, and established Nagato as a regional power that rivalled the Honda clan in the early Sengoku period. The mine was finally exhausted in 1973, but the {{nihongo|Kumagawa Mining Museum|熊川鉱山博物館|Kumagawa Kōzan Hakubutsukan}} preserves the heritage, and the mine shafts have been repurposed as a geothermal energy source.

=== The washi tradition ===
[[File:Nagato Washi Workshop.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A traditional Nagato washi paper workshop]]
Nagato's {{nihongo|washi|和紙}} paper-making tradition dates to the 8th century, when Buddhist monks established the first paper workshops in the [[Hayase]] river valley. The {{nihongo|Nagato Washi|長門和紙|Nagato Washi}} is made from the inner bark of the {{nihongo|''kōzo''|楮}} mulberry tree, which grows abundantly on the prefecture's mountain slopes, and the clean, soft water of the Hayase River produces a paper of exceptional strength and translucency.

The {{nihongo|Nagato Paper Guild|長門紙組合|Nagato Kami Kumiai}}, established in 1392, is the oldest continuously operating artisanal cooperative in Okaiken. The Guild's products—ranging from calligraphy paper to shoji screen panels to conservation-grade archival paper—command premium prices, and Nagato washi is used in the restoration of [[w:UNESCO|UNESCO]] World Heritage documents across Adonia. The industry employs approximately 2,800 artisans and generates approximately 円6 billion in annual revenue.

== Geography ==

Nagato Prefecture occupies the southern coast of Yagikoshu, facing the Shendan Ocean to the south. The prefecture is bounded by [[Kagami Prefecture]] to the east, [[Obama Prefecture]] and [[Nagayama Prefecture]] to the north, and [[Mizuno Prefecture]] to the west. The prefecture's geography is defined by the contrast between the rugged southern coastline—where sea cliffs up to 200 metres high alternate with small, sheltered fishing bays—and the mountainous interior, where the [[Nagato Range]] rises to over 1,400 metres.

The [[Hayase River]] is the prefecture's primary waterway, draining the northern mountains and providing the clean water essential for the washi industry. The [[Kumagawa River]] flows through the former mining district, its waters still tinged with trace copper minerals that give the river a distinctive blue-green colour.

=== Climate ===
Nagato Prefecture has a [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] (''Cfa''), with warm, humid summers and mild winters. The southern coast receives the full force of the Shendan Ocean's weather systems, and the prefecture experiences significant rainfall (over 3,200 mm annually) and occasional typhoon impacts. The mountainous interior is cooler, with winter snowfall at higher elevations.

