== Economy ==

Nagato Prefecture's economy is driven by washi paper production, copper-related industries, and ocean fishing. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円30 billion in 2025, with industry accounting for 38%, services 42%, and agriculture/fisheries 20%.

=== Washi paper ===
The {{nihongo|Nagato Washi|長門和紙}} industry is described above. In addition to traditional hand-made paper, the prefecture has developed a significant industrial paper sector, producing high-performance filter paper, insulation paper for electronics, and conservation-grade archival materials. The {{nihongo|Nagato Paper Technology Centre|長門紙技術センター|Nagato Kami Gijutsu Sentā}} conducts research into advanced paper applications, including biodegradable electronics substrates and medical-grade filtration media.

=== Copper and minerals ===
Although the Kumagawa mine is exhausted, the prefecture's copper heritage has been repurposed in several ways. The {{nihongo|Kumagawa Copper Refinery|熊川銅精錬所|Kumagawa Dō Seirensho}}, which operated from 1572 to 1973, has been converted into a heritage museum and geothermal power station, using the hot water that now fills the mine shafts to generate approximately 120 MW of electricity. The refinery's copper-smelting expertise has been transferred to the {{nihongo|Nagato Non-Ferrous Metals Zone|長門非鉄金属地帯|Nagato Hitetsu Kinzoku Chitai}}, which processes imported copper and other non-ferrous metals for the electronics and automotive industries.

=== Fugu and ocean fishing ===
[[File:Nagato Fugu Chef.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A licensed Nagato fugu chef preparing pufferfish sashimi]]
The Shendan Ocean off Nagato's coast is one of Okaiken's prime {{nihongo|fugu|河豚}} (pufferfish) fishing grounds, and the prefecture is the nation's leading producer of this notoriously dangerous delicacy. The {{nihongo|Nagato Fugu|長門河豚|Nagato Fugu}} is prized for its firm texture and delicate flavour, and the prefecture's licensed fugu chefs undergo a rigorous three-year apprenticeship before they are permitted to prepare the fish. The Nagato Fugu Association oversees the industry, which generates approximately 円4.5 billion in annual revenue.

The prefecture's fishing fleet also lands significant quantities of sea bream, mackerel, and squid, with total annual landings of approximately 70,000 tonnes.
Nagato preserves Okaiken's most celebrated washi papermaking traditions along the pristine upper reaches of its river systems. Master papermakers of the Okami-gami guild cultivate native kozo mulberry bushes, harvesting and hand-beating the inner bark fibers on river boulders before dipping fine bamboo screens into freezing spring water mixed with natural mallow root mucilage. The resulting archival washi paper is renowned for its strength, translucent luster, and chemical durability, utilized for sacred Shoto talismans, legal charters, and museum restoration.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Okaiken Railways]] [[Nagato Line]] connects [[Nagato Station]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]] in approximately 140 minutes on express services. The [[Shendan Coast Line]] runs along the southern coast.

=== Air ===
[[Nagato Airport]] (IATA: NGO) operates domestic flights to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]].

=== Port ===
The [[Port of Nagato]] handles approximately 5 million tonnes of cargo annually and operates ferry services to [[Harujima]].

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Nagato's cuisine is defined by the Shendan Ocean and the mountains. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''Nagato fugu''|長門河豚}}—pufferfish, served as {{nihongo|''sashimi''|刺身}} (thinly sliced raw), {{nihongo|''chiri-nabe''|ちり鍋}} (hot pot), and {{nihongo|''hirezake''|ヒレ酒}} (fin sake). Other specialities include {{nihongo|''nagato-soba''|長門蕎麦}} (buckwheat noodles made with washi-filtered water), and {{nihongo|''kumagawa-dō yaki''|熊川銅焼}}—copper-coloured grilled fish, named for the mine.

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Nagato Fugu Festival'''|長門河豚祭り|Nagato Fugu Matsuri}} — Annual pufferfish festival (February), drawing approximately 80,000 visitors
* {{nihongo|'''Kumagawa Mine Festival'''|熊川鉱山祭り|Kumagawa Kōzan Matsuri}} — Annual heritage festival (October), celebrating the copper mining tradition
* {{nihongo|'''Washi Festival'''|和紙祭り|Washi Matsuri}} — Annual paper-making festival (November), showcasing the Nagato washi tradition

